# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`VaultManagerV2::withdraw` Checks if the last deposit to the DNft id was in the same block, if it was, then it reverts. A malicious user can call `VaultManagerV2::deposit` with any amount of asset to someone else's DNft id in the same block front-running the withdraw call, therefore making it impossible to withdraw.

## Proof of Concept
```  
  function withdraw(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  ) 
    public
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    if (idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id] == block.number) revert DepositedInSameBlock();
```
The `withdraw` function starts with a check, if the last deposit into the DNft id happened in the same block, if it did, the function reverts. This check is implemented against flash loan attacks, however, it can be used by a malicious user to block withdraw by front-running it.

```
  function deposit(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount
  ) 
    external 
      isValidDNft(id)
  {
    idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id] = block.number;
    Vault _vault = Vault(vault);
    _vault.asset().safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(vault), amount);
    _vault.deposit(id, amount);
  }
```
The `deposit` function only has an `isValidDNft(id)` modifier on it, not the `isDNftOwner(id)` modifier, so anybody can call it, as it only checks if the given id is valid.

```
  modifier isValidDNft(uint id) {
    if (dNft.ownerOf(id) == address(0))   revert InvalidDNft(); _;
  }
```
Then the `deposit` saves the `block.number` to the `idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id]`, so the `withdraw` function call will revert if it is called in the same block after the `deposit` function.
