# Vulnerability details

## Summary
A user can only burn `DYAD` tokens which they themselves minted with a particular `dNFT` they own given by `id`. During minting of `DYAD` tokens, a mapping `mintedDyad[msg.sender][id]` is created and updated by incrementing by `amount` in the `dyad.mint()`.

On the other hand, during burning of `DYAD` tokens, this mapping is updated by decrementing by `amount` being burned. It only follows that there must be an existing record of `DYAD` tokens minted for the specified `dNFT (id)` and owner (`msg.sender`) for this to execute.

However, in the `liquidate()` function, the parameters passed to the `dyad.burn()` function call are mixed up and will only result in this call failing.

## Proof of Concept
`Liquidation Process`:
In the liquidation process, the `liquidator` (the caller of the `liquidate()` function) is required to burn an equivalent amount of `DYAD` tokens to the total amount minted by the `liquidatee` (the owner of the `dNFT with ID id`).

